# Architecture documentation sync

When you change **system topology**, **cross-service boundaries**, or **major request paths**, update **`docs/architecture/`** in the **same PR** (or a tightly linked follow-up) so the site stays accurate.

## In scope (update architecture docs when you touch)

- **New or removed runtime components** (e.g. new services, routers under **`api/`**, MCP transports, background workers).
- **Request / data flows** that agents or operators rely on (LLM events, OCR upload → recognize → relay, MCP → HTTP mapping).
- **Shared infrastructure** (Redis idempotency, image store, GPU vs CPU inference notes, Compose/Kubernetes wiring that changes ports or dependencies).
- **`utils/ocr/`** pipeline shape (stages, parallelism, failure partial-success semantics).
- **`jarvis_mcp`** process model (**stdio** vs **streamable-http** vs **both**).

## What to update

1. **`docs/architecture/index.md`** (primary) — deployment diagram, sequence workflows, tables for components and data boundaries. Add or adjust **Mermaid** diagrams when flows change (keep node IDs **alphanumeric**; follow Material/Mermaid constraints).
2. **`mkdocs.yml`** — if you add a new page under **`docs/architecture/`**, register it under **Architecture** in **`nav`**.
3. Run **`mkdocs build --strict`** — architecture pages must not introduce broken internal links (same bar as **`Dockerfile.docs`**).

Also align **narrow normative specs** (e.g. **`docs/services/ocr.md`**) and this **architecture** overview: architecture explains *why and how things connect*; service specs remain *contractual*.

Cross-rule: general MkDocs hygiene is **`.cursor/rules/docs-and-build-sync.mdc`**.
